      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the complication rates of unilateral and bilateral varicose vein surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Observational clinical study with prospective data collection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Southport and Ormskirk Hospital NHS Trust."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "1090 patients undergoing varicose vein surgery during the period January 2002-June 2005. 695 (64%) of these had unilateral surgery and 395 (36%) had bilateral surgery giving a total of 1485 legs operated on (47% as a unilateral operation and 53% as a bilateral operation)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "43/695 (6.2%) of patients who underwent unilateral surgery and 34/395 (8.6%) of patients who underwent bilateral surgery developed a wound infection. 77/695 (11%) of patients who underwent unilateral surgery developed paraesthesia compared to 81/395 (21%) for bilateral surgery. 2/695 (0.13%) unilateral surgery patients and no bilateral surgery patients developed neuralgia. Residual varicose veins were present in 30/695 (4.3%) of unilateral group and 32/395 (8.1%) of the bilateral group. 1/695 (0.14%) of unilateral surgery patients and 3/395 (0.75%) of bilateral surgery patients developed DVTs. Comparing limbs, a complication of any type was seen in 153/695 (22%) limbs treated by unilateral surgery and 150/790 (19% N.S., Chi squared) limbs treated by bilateral surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Complications following varicose vein surgery appear to be more common following bilateral procedures. However, the complication rate per limb is similar for unilateral and bilateral operations."
        }
      ]},
